Task 1: Establishing a Sequential Baseline
Subtask 1.1: Inspect Default Parallelism Settings

    Check the current fork and strategy configuration:

ansible-config dump | grep -i fork
ansible-config dump | grep -i strategy

Expected output shows DEFAULT_FORKS(default) = 5 and DEFAULT_STRATEGY(default) = linear. This confirms Ansible runs against at most 5 hosts concurrently using the linear strategy unless overridden.
Subtask 1.2: Build and Time a Sequential Playbook

Sequential timing must be measured from outside the playbook, using the shell's time command, so the measurement reflects real wall-clock elapsed time rather than a value computed inside a single task.

    Create the sequential playbook:

cat > sequential-playbook.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Sequential Task Execution Baseline
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: yes
  serial: 1

  tasks:
    - name: Install packages sequentially
      yum:
        name:
          - htop
          - tree
          - wget
          - curl
        state: present
      become: yes

    - name: Create multiple directories
      file:
        path: "/tmp/test_dir_{{ item }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      loop:
        - 1
        - 2
        - 3
        - 4
        - 5

    - name: Download test files
      get_url:
        url: "https://httpbin.org/delay/2"
        dest: "/tmp/test_file_{{ inventory_hostname }}.json"
        timeout: 30

    - name: Simulate processing time
      command: sleep 3
      changed_when: false
EOF

    Execute and capture the elapsed time using the shell, redirecting the time output to a file so it can be parsed later:

{ time ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini sequential-playbook.yml ; } 2> sequential_time.log
cat sequential_time.log

Deliverable: sequential_time.log contains a real, user, sys line block, for example real 0m47.312s. Record the real value; this is your sequential baseline (expect roughly 40-55 seconds with serial: 1 across 4 hosts, since each host is fully processed before the next begins).
Task 2: Implementing Parallel Execution Strategies
Subtask 2.1: Configure Fork-Based Parallelism

    Create ansible.cfg with increased forks and SSH optimizations:

cat > ansible.cfg << 'EOF'
[defaults]
inventory = inventory.ini
host_key_checking = False
forks = 10
timeout = 30
gathering = smart
fact_caching = memory

[ssh_connection]
ssh_args = -o ControlMaster=auto -o ControlPersist=60s
pipelining = True
EOF

    Create a parallel playbook that runs the identical workload under the linear strategy, benefiting from higher forks (all 4 hosts execute each task simultaneously instead of one at a time):

cat > parallel-forks-playbook.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Parallel Task Execution with Forks
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: yes
  strategy: linear

  tasks:
    - name: Install packages in parallel
      yum:
        name:
          - htop
          - tree
          - wget
          - curl
        state: present
      become: yes

    - name: Create multiple directories in parallel
      file:
        path: "/tmp/parallel_dir_{{ item }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      loop:
        - 1
        - 2
        - 3
        - 4
        - 5

    - name: Download test files in parallel
      get_url:
        url: "https://httpbin.org/delay/2"
        dest: "/tmp/parallel_file_{{ inventory_hostname }}.json"
        timeout: 30

    - name: Simulate processing time
      command: sleep 3
      changed_when: false
EOF

    Execute and capture timing the same way:

{ time ansible-playbook parallel-forks-playbook.yml ; } 2> parallel_time.log
cat parallel_time.log

Deliverable: parallel_time.log shows a real time noticeably lower than sequential_time.log (expect roughly 12-16 seconds, since all 4 hosts now execute each task concurrently instead of sequentially).
Subtask 2.2: Implement Free Strategy for Maximum Parallelism

The free strategy allows each host to progress through the task list independently, without waiting for slower hosts to catch up on each task.

    Create the free-strategy playbook:

cat > free-strategy-playbook.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Free Strategy Parallel Execution
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: yes
  strategy: free

  tasks:
    - name: Install packages with free strategy
      yum:
        name:
          - htop
          - tree
          - wget
          - curl
          - vim
        state: present
      become: yes

    - name: Create directories independently
      file:
        path: "/tmp/free_dir_{{ item }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      loop:
        - 1
        - 2
        - 3
        - 4
        - 5
        - 6
        - 7

    - name: Download files independently
      get_url:
        url: "https://httpbin.org/delay/2"
        dest: "/tmp/free_file_{{ inventory_hostname }}.json"
        timeout: 30

    - name: Variable processing time per host
      command: "sleep {{ (ansible_play_hosts.index(inventory_hostname) | int) + 2 }}"
      changed_when: false
EOF

    Execute and time it:

{ time ansible-playbook free-strategy-playbook.yml ; } 2> free_time.log
cat free_time.log

Deliverable: free_time.log shows a real time similar to or slightly better than parallel_time.log, since faster hosts no longer wait for node1's longer sleep before moving to the next task.
Subtask 2.3: Optimize with Asynchronous Tasks

    Create an asynchronous playbook where long tasks run in the background while other work proceeds:

cat > async-playbook.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Asynchronous Task Execution
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Start long-running task asynchronously
      command: "sleep {{ 10 + (ansible_play_hosts.index(inventory_hostname) | int) * 2 }}"
      async: 30
      poll: 0
      register: long_task

    - name: Start file download asynchronously
      get_url:
        url: "https://httpbin.org/delay/5"
        dest: "/tmp/async_download_{{ inventory_hostname }}.json"
        timeout: 60
      async: 70
      poll: 0
      register: download_task

    - name: Perform other work while async tasks run
      file:
        path: "/tmp/async_work_{{ item }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      loop:
        - 1
        - 2
        - 3

    - name: Install packages while waiting
      yum:
        name:
          - nc
          - telnet
        state: present
      become: yes

    - name: Wait for long-running task to finish
      async_status:
        jid: "{{ long_task.ansible_job_id }}"
      register: long_task_result
      until: long_task_result.finished
      retries: 30
      delay: 1

    - name: Wait for download task to finish
      async_status:
        jid: "{{ download_task.ansible_job_id }}"
      register: download_result
      until: download_result.finished
      retries: 70
      delay: 1

    - name: Display results
      debug:
        msg:
          - "Long task rc: {{ long_task_result.rc | default('N/A') }}"
          - "Download task rc: {{ download_result.rc | default('N/A') }}"
EOF

    Execute and time it:

{ time ansible-playbook async-playbook.yml ; } 2> async_time.log
cat async_time.log

Deliverable: async_time.log shows real time close to the duration of the longest async task rather than the sum of all task durations, since the "other work" tasks and package installs run while the sleep and download continue in the background.
Task 3: Measuring and Comparing Performance Accurately
Subtask 3.1: Build a Correct Timing Harness

Ansible facts such as ansible_date_time.epoch are captured once per host during the gather_facts step at the start of a play. Using set_fact with lookup('pipe', 'date +%s') inside the same task block does not measure elapsed time between two points in a playbook â�� it only captures the instant that specific task runs. To get a reliable, reproducible measurement, timing must be done outside Ansible, wrapping the entire ansible-playbook invocation with the shell time builtin (as done in Tasks 1 and 2), or by writing start/end timestamps to a file from the control node before and after invoking ansible-playbook.

    Create a shared workload tasks file used by every comparison run:

cat > workload-tasks.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: CPU intensive task
  command: "dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/test_{{ test_name }}_{{ inventory_hostname }} bs=1M count=50"
  changed_when: false

- name: Create test directories
  file:
    path: "/tmp/{{ test_name }}_dir_{{ item }}_{{ inventory_hostname }}"
    state: directory
  loop:
    - 1
    - 2
    - 3

- name: Network operation simulation
  uri:
    url: "https://httpbin.org/delay/1"
    method: GET
    timeout: 10
  ignore_errors: yes

- name: Cleanup test file
  file:
    path: "/tmp/test_{{ test_name }}_{{ inventory_hostname }}"
    state: absent
EOF

    Create three minimal playbooks that each run only the workload, under a different strategy, with no internal timing logic:

cat > compare-sequential.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Performance Comparison - Sequential
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: no
  serial: 1
  vars:
    test_name: "sequential"
  tasks:
    - include_tasks: workload-tasks.yml
EOF

cat > compare-linear.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Performance Comparison - Linear
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: no
  strategy: linear
  vars:
    test_name: "linear"
  tasks:
    - include_tasks: workload-tasks.yml
EOF

cat > compare-free.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Performance Comparison - Free
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: no
  strategy: free
  vars:
    test_name: "free"
  tasks:
    - include_tasks: workload-tasks.yml
EOF

Subtask 3.2: Run the Comparison and Compute Real Improvement Percentages

    Create a driver script that wraps each playbook with the shell time command, extracts the real seconds using jc, and computes improvement percentages against the sequential baseline:

cat > run_comparison.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

run_and_time() {
  local playbook=$1
  local logfile=$2
  { /usr/bin/time -p ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini "$playbook" > /dev/null ; } 2> "$logfile"
  grep '^real' "$logfile" | awk '{print $2}'
}

echo "Running sequential baseline..."
SEQ_TIME=$(run_and_time compare-sequential.yml seq_raw.log)

echo "Running linear strategy..."
LIN_TIME=$(run_and_time compare-linear.yml lin_raw.log)

echo "Running free strategy..."
FREE_TIME=$(run_and_time compare-free.yml free_raw.log)

echo "=== PERFORMANCE COMPARISON RESULTS ===" | tee comparison_results.txt
echo "Sequential execution time: ${SEQ_TIME}s" | tee -a comparison_results.txt
echo "Linear execution time:     ${LIN_TIME}s" | tee -a comparison_results.txt
echo "Free execution time:       ${FREE_TIME}s" | tee -a comparison_results.txt

python3 - "$SEQ_TIME" "$LIN_TIME" "$FREE_TIME" << 'PYEOF' | tee -a comparison_results.txt
import sys
seq, lin, free = (float(x) for x in sys.argv[1:4])
lin_improvement = ((seq - lin) / seq) * 100
free_improvement = ((seq - free) / seq) * 100
print(f"Linear improvement over sequential: {lin_improvement:.2f}%")
print(f"Free strategy improvement over sequential: {free_improvement:.2f}%")
PYEOF

echo "=== END RESULTS ===" | tee -a comparison_results.txt
EOF

chmod +x run_comparison.sh
./run_comparison.sh

Deliverable: comparison_results.txt contains three real elapsed times and two improvement percentages, for example:

=== PERFORMANCE COMPARISON RESULTS ===
Sequential execution time: 41.20s
Linear execution time:     13.85s
Free execution time:       11.60s
Linear improvement over sequential: 66.38%
Free strategy improvement over sequential: 71.84%
=== END RESULTS ===

Because every timing figure now comes from the shell time command wrapped around the entire ansible-playbook invocation, these percentages reflect genuine wall-clock improvement rather than an artifact of when a set_fact task happened to execute.
Subtask 3.3: Verify Race-Condition Handling Under Parallelism

Free-strategy and high-fork execution can expose race conditions when multiple hosts write to a shared resource. Demonstrate and then correct this.

    Run a playbook where all hosts append to one shared file on node1 without serialization:

cat > race-condition-demo.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Demonstrate Race Condition
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: yes
  strategy: free

  tasks:
    - name: Ensure shared resource exists
      file:
        path: "/tmp/shared_resource.txt"
        state: touch
      delegate_to: "{{ groups['webservers'][0] }}"
      run_once: true

    - name: Concurrent writers without locking
      lineinfile:
        path: "/tmp/shared_resource.txt"
        line: "{{ inventory_hostname }} wrote at {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}"
        create: yes
      delegate_to: "{{ groups['webservers'][0] }}"
EOF

ansible-playbook race-condition-demo.yml

    Retrieve the shared file with a correctly formed fetch command (use one module flag per invocation, never combine -m command and -m fetch in the same call):

ansible node1 -i inventory.ini -m fetch -a "src=/tmp/shared_resource.txt dest=./fetched_shared_resource.txt flat=yes"
cat fetched_shared_resource.txt

If you only need to view the file contents remotely without copying it locally, use the command module on its own instead:

ansible node1 -i inventory.ini -m command -a "cat /tmp/shared_resource.txt"

    Now apply throttle: 1 to serialize writes and confirm every host's line appears exactly once, in order:

cat > race-condition-fixed.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Fixed Concurrent Write Handling
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: yes
  strategy: free

  tasks:
    - name: Reset shared resource
      file:
        path: "/tmp/shared_resource_fixed.txt"
        state: touch
      delegate_to: "{{ groups['webservers'][0] }}"
      run_once: true

    - name: Serialized writers using throttle
      lineinfile:
        path: "/tmp/shared_resource_fixed.txt"
        line: "{{ inventory_hostname }} wrote at {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}"
      delegate_to: "{{ groups['webservers'][0] }}"
      throttle: 1
EOF

ansible-playbook race-condition-fixed.yml
ansible node1 -i inventory.ini -m fetch -a "src=/tmp/shared_resource_fixed.txt dest=./fetched_shared_resource_fixed.txt flat=yes"
cat fetched_shared_resource_fixed.txt

Deliverable: fetched_shared_resource_fixed.txt contains exactly four lines, one per host (node1 through node4), with no missing or corrupted entries, demonstrating that throttle: 1 eliminated the race condition present in the unthrottled version.
Expected Outcomes

    A set of working playbooks demonstrating sequential, linear-parallel, free-strategy, and asynchronous execution against the same workload
    A comparison_results.txt file containing accurate wall-clock timings and correctly computed improvement percentages for linear and free strategies versus the sequential baseline
    Verified, reproducible evidence that throttle: 1 resolves a race condition on a shared resource that otherwise occurs under the free strategy

Troubleshooting

    If your comparison_results.txt shows the free strategy performing worse than linear, investigate: how many forks does your ansible.cfg currently allow, and is that number smaller than the number of hosts in your inventory? What happens if you raise forks and rerun run_comparison.sh?
    If fetched_shared_resource_fixed.txt is missing lines from one or more hosts, check whether delegate_to in your playbook actually points at a single consistent host across all runs, and whether any earlier failed run left stale lock files or partially written data in /tmp on the delegated host. What would you inspect first to confirm the root cause?

Conclusion

In this lab, students moved from a naive sequential Ansible baseline to multiple parallel execution strategies, including increased forks with the linear strategy, the free strategy, and asynchronous task execution, applying each to an identical workload for a fair comparison. Students then built a timing harness that measures elapsed wall-clock time from outside the playbook using the shell time command rather than relying on in-playbook fact timestamps, producing trustworthy improvement percentages instead of misleading ones. Finally, students reproduced a real race condition caused by unsynchronized concurrent writes under the free strategy and resolved it using throttle: 1, verifying the fix by fetching and inspecting the shared file with correctly formed ansible ad hoc commands. Going forward, students should apply this same rigor, measuring outside the automation tool and validating shared-resource access, whenever tuning parallelism in production Ansible environments.
